WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.760
Plot the pressure inside the left ventricle against its volume.

2
00:00:07.340 --> 00:00:09.640
Each heartbeat then draws a closed loop.

3
00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:13.720
This is a model adult ventricle at rest, with made-up numbers.

4
00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:16.880
It has four corners, and an area inside.

5
00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:20.260
When the arteries tighten, which corner moves?

6
00:00:21.233 --> 00:00:23.113
The beat runs counterclockwise.

7
00:00:23.553 --> 00:00:26.313
At the bottom right, the mitral valve closes.

8
00:00:26.933 --> 00:00:31.653
That volume is the end diastolic volume, 125 millilitres.

9
00:00:32.193 --> 00:00:34.313
Pressure now rises with the volume fixed.

10
00:00:34.793 --> 00:00:40.653
At about 80 millimetres of mercury, the aortic valve opens and the ventricle ejects.

11
00:00:40.933 --> 00:00:43.673
At the top left, the aortic valve closes.

12
00:00:43.673 --> 00:00:50.953
That volume is the end systolic volume, 45 millilitres at 120 millimetres of

13
00:00:50.953 --> 00:00:54.473
mercury. Pressure then falls with the volume fixed.

14
00:00:55.093 --> 00:00:57.853
The mitral valve opens, and the ventricle fills.

15
00:00:59.383 --> 00:01:01.483
The width of the loop is the stroke volume.

16
00:01:02.083 --> 00:01:05.503
125 minus 45 is 80 millilitres.

17
00:01:06.163 --> 00:01:10.703
Divide 80 by 125, and the ejection fraction is 64 percent.

18
00:01:11.223 --> 00:01:14.243
The area inside the loop is the stroke work of the beat.

19
00:01:15.067 --> 00:01:19.527
The top left corner lies on a line, the end-systolic pressure-volume relation.

20
00:01:20.007 --> 00:01:25.127
Its slope, Ees, is a relatively load-independent index of contractility.

21
00:01:25.547 --> 00:01:30.547
Here Ees is three millimetres of mercury per millilitre, and the line meets the

22
00:01:30.547 --> 00:01:32.587
volume axis at five millilitres.

23
00:01:32.967 --> 00:01:36.867
A second line starts at the end-diastolic volume and runs to the corner.

24
00:01:37.447 --> 00:01:42.887
Its steepness is the effective arterial elastance, Ea, here 1.5.

25
00:01:43.467 --> 00:01:45.507
The corner sits where the two lines meet.

26
00:01:47.267 --> 00:01:51.667
Now tighten the arteries, and hold filling and contractility fixed.

27
00:01:52.327 --> 00:01:56.347
In the model, arteriolar constriction raises Ea to 2.

28
00:01:56.907 --> 00:02:00.407
The steeper line meets the same end-systolic line higher up.

29
00:02:00.967 --> 00:02:05.267
end-systolic pressure rises to 144 millimetres of mercury.

30
00:02:05.967 --> 00:02:09.207
end-systolic volume rises to 53 millilitres.

31
00:02:09.827 --> 00:02:11.247
Stroke volume falls to 72.

32
00:02:11.247 --> 00:02:15.107
Ejection fraction falls to 57.6 percent.

33
00:02:15.817 --> 00:02:19.837
Filling and contractility did not change, yet ejection fraction fell.

34
00:02:20.457 --> 00:02:23.897
So ejection fraction can change with no change in contractility.

35
00:02:24.397 --> 00:02:25.497
Go back to the first loop.

36
00:02:26.217 --> 00:02:30.557
Raise filling to 140 millilitres and the corner slides up the same line.

37
00:02:31.217 --> 00:02:32.477
Stroke volume rises to 90.

38
00:02:33.017 --> 00:02:38.197
Now return filling to 125, and raise Ees to 4.5.

39
00:02:38.597 --> 00:02:42.337
The line steepens, and end systolic volume falls to 35.

40
00:02:43.057 --> 00:02:48.637
The rule to keep: preload, afterload and contractility each move a different part of the loop.
